L000289	\subsection{S1.3. Mass Conservation and Burial}
L000290	
L000291	{We use the physical model of burial developed in \cite{costello2021secondary} and illustrated in Fig.~\ref{fig:Schematic}, ensuring} volumetric consistency between material removed from the bowl of a crater and relocated as ejecta. Here, we adapt this treatment for our four discrete depositional annuli (labeled A through D). By equating a deposited volume fraction $P_{V,k}$ to the geometric volume of its receiving annulus, we derive the dimensionless burial scale $h_{b,k}$:
L000292	\begin{equation}
L000293	h_{b,k} = \frac{P_{V,k}}{18(j_k^2 - i_k^2)}
L000294	\end{equation}
L000295	where $i_k$ and $j_k$ are the inner and outer radii of the annulus in units of the transient crater radius $R$. Unlike the bulk excavation and compaction zones, the net burial is a superposition of transport from multiple spatial interaction scales $c_{b,k} = \sqrt{j_k^2 - i_k^2}$.
L000296	
L000297	By substituting these scales into the process-specific transport law, Eq. (\ref{eq:AnalyticGardening}), the total burial depth $D_B(t)$ is the sum of contributions from all ejecta annuli:
L000298	\begin{equation}
L000299	\label{eq:Burial_Full}
L000300	\begin{split}
L000301	D_B(t) &= \sum_k D_{b,k}(t) \\
L000302	&= \underbrace{ \left[ \sum_{k \in \{A, B, C, D\}} \frac{h_{b,k}}{c_{b,k}^{\frac{2}{-b+2}}} \right] }_{\text{Geometric Efficiency}} \underbrace{ \left( \frac{4(b-2)\lambda}{  \pi a b  t c_{i}^2} \right)^{\frac{1}{b-2}} }_{\text{Probabilistic Frequency}}
L000303	\end{split}
L000304	\end{equation}
L000305	
L000306	This allows us to define an effective burial efficiency,
L000307	\begin{equation}
L000308	\eta_b(b) = \sum_{k \in \{A, B, C, D\}} \frac{h_{b,k}}{c_{b,k}^{\frac{2}{-b+2}}},
L000309	\end{equation}
L000310	\noindent representing the net vertical reach of the combined ejecta blanket. To evaluate $\eta_b(b)$ explicitly in terms of the fundamental parameters of the annuli, we substitute $h_{b,k} = \frac{P_{V,k}}{18(j_k^2 - i_k^2)}$ and $c_{b,k} = \sqrt{j_k^2 - i_k^2}$ into the summation:
L000311	\begin{equation}
L000312	    \eta_b(b) = \sum_{k \in \{A, B, C, D\}} \frac{\frac{P_{V,k}}{18(j_k^2 - i_k^2)}}{\left( \sqrt{j_k^2 - i_k^2} \right)^{\frac{2}{-b+2}}}
L000313	\end{equation}
L000314	
L000315	Consolidating the terms in the denominator, the $(j_k^2 - i_k^2)$ factors combine as $(j_k^2 - i_k^2)^1 \cdot (j_k^2 - i_k^2)^{\frac{1}{-b+2}} = (j_k^2 - i_k^2)^{\frac{-b+3}{-b+2}}$. This yields the final closed-form solution for the effective burial efficiency:
L000316	\begin{equation}
L000317	\label{eq:EtaBurial}
L000318	    \eta_b(b) = \frac{1}{18} \sum_{k \in \{A, B, C, D\}} \frac{P_{V,k}}{\left( j_k^2 - i_k^2 \right)^{\frac{-b+3}{-b+2}}}
L000319	\end{equation}
L000320	
L000321	The dimensionless inner radii $i_k$, outer radii $j_k$, and depositional volume fractions $P_{V,k}$ for each annulus are detailed in Table \ref{tab:model_summary}. For a secondary-dominated impactor population ($b \approx -4$ \cite{costello2021secondary}), evaluating this summation using these parameters yields $\eta_b \approx 0.032$, which we use as the standard burial efficiency in our advection calculations.
